    "title": "Aurora and Milky Way in a Little Sky",
    "permalink": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2014-october-3-aurora-and-milky-way-in-a-little-sky/",
    "media_type": "image",
    "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> Stepping stones seem to lead to the Milky Way as it stretches across this <a href=\"http://www.subblue.com/blog/2010/6/17/little_planets\" target=\"_blank\">little sky</a> . Of course, the scene is really the northern hemisphere's autumnal <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap140922.html\">equinox</a> night. Water and sky are inverted by a top to bottom, around the horizon <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ Stereographic_projection#Photography\" target=\"_blank\">stereographic projection</a> centered on the zenith above Lake Storsjön in Jämtland, Sweden. In the north the Milky Way arcs from east to west overhead as fall begins, <a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/mission_pages/themis/auroras/ aurora_live.html\" target=\"_blank\">but the season</a> is also a good time for viewing aurora. <a href=\"http://science.nasa.gov/science-news/science-at-nasa/2012/ 02mar_aurorasunderfoot/\" target=\"_blank\">Geomagnetic storms</a> increase in frequency near the equinox and produce remarkable displays of northern lights at high latitudes, like the eerie greenish glow reflected in this watery cosmos.",
    "credit": "<b>Image Credit &amp; <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/lib/about_apod.html#srapply\">Copyright</a>:</b> <a href=\"http://www.astrofotografen.se/\" target=\"_blank\">Göran Strand</a>",
    "copyright": "<b>Image Credit &amp; <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/lib/about_apod.html#srapply\">Copyright</a>:</b> <a href=\"http://www.astrofotografen.se/\" target=\"_blank\">Göran Strand</a>",
    "alt": "See Explanation. Clicking on the picture will download the highest resolution version available.",
    "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2014-october-3-aurora-and-milky-way-in-a-little-sky/",
    "hdurl": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2014/october/GS_20140924_Aurora_6496_Pan2.jpg?w=1000&h=1000&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint",
    "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2014 October 3 - Aurora and Milky Way in a Little Sky</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2014-october-3-aurora-and-milky-way-in-a-little-sky/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Robert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: Stepping stones seem to lead to the Milky Way as it stretches across this little sky . Of course, the scene is really the northern hemisphere&#039;s autumnal equinox night. Water and sky are inverted by a top to bottom, around the horizon stereographic projection centered on the zenith above Lake Storsjön in Jämtland, Sweden. In the north the Milky Way arcs from east to west overhead as fall begins, but the season is also a good time for viewing aurora. Geomagnetic storms increase in frequency near the equinox and produce remarkable displays of northern lights at high latitudes, like the eerie greenish glow reflected in this watery cosmos.\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"aurora and milky way in a little sky\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"Aurora and Milky Way in a Little Sky\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2014-october-3-aurora-and-milky-way-in-a-little-sky/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2014/october/GS_20140924_Aurora_6496_Pan2.jpg?w=1000&#038;h=1000&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2014/october/GS_20140924_Aurora_6496_Pan2.jpg?w=1000&#038;h=1000&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"See Explanation. Clicking on the picture will download the highest resolution version available.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2014-10-03T00:00:00-04:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-07-29T13:42:52-04:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"Aurora and Milky Way in a Little Sky\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2014/october/GS_20140924_Aurora_6496_Pan2.jpg?w=1000&#038;h=1000&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2014 October 3<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2014/october/GS_20140924_Aurora_6496_Pan2.jpg\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2014/october/GS_20140924_Aurora_6496_Pan2.jpg\" alt=\"See Explanation. Clicking on the picture will download the highest resolution version available.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> Aurora and Milky Way in a Little Sky </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<b>Image Credit &amp; <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/lib/about_apod.html#srapply\">Copyright</a>:</b> <a href=\"http://www.astrofotografen.se/\" target=\"_blank\">Göran Strand</a>\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> Stepping stones seem to lead to the Milky Way as it stretches across this <a href=\"http://www.subblue.com/blog/2010/6/17/little_planets\" target=\"_blank\">little sky</a> . Of course, the scene is really the northern hemisphere's autumnal <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap140922.html\">equinox</a> night. Water and sky are inverted by a top to bottom, around the horizon <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ Stereographic_projection#Photography\" target=\"_blank\">stereographic projection</a> centered on the zenith above Lake Storsjön in Jämtland, Sweden. In the north the Milky Way arcs from east to west overhead as fall begins, <a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/mission_pages/themis/auroras/ aurora_live.html\" target=\"_blank\">but the season</a> is also a good time for viewing aurora. <a href=\"http://science.nasa.gov/science-news/science-at-nasa/2012/ 02mar_aurorasunderfoot/\" target=\"_blank\">Geomagnetic storms</a> increase in frequency near the equinox and produce remarkable displays of northern lights at high latitudes, like the eerie greenish glow reflected in this watery cosmos.\n<p> <center>\n\n<b> Tomorrow's picture: </b><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2014-october-4-mars-antares-moon-and-saturn/\">APOD: 2014 October 4 &#8211; Mars, Antares, Moon, and Saturn</a>\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2014-october-2-the-bubble-nebula/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2014-october-4-mars-antares-moon-and-saturn/\">&gt;</a>\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\n<a href=\"https://www.mtu.edu/physics/department/faculty/nemiroff/\" target=\"_blank\">Robert Nemiroff</a> (<a href=\"https://www.mtu.edu/physics/\" target=\"_blank\">MTU</a>) &amp; <a href=\"https://inspirehep.net/authors/1073087\" target=\"_blank\">Jerry Bonnell</a> (<a href=\"https://www.astro.umd.edu/\" target=\"_blank\">UMCP</a>)<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>",
    "basic_html_url": "https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/141003/html"
